//@ expect: accept
//@ reason: `throw` accepts a `+` concatenation of string-valued operands (a string
//@ reason: literal, a string-typed identifier, or a nested concatenation), not just
//@ reason: a bare literal/identifier -- string concatenation has no ownership or
//@ reason: dangling implications (always produces a fresh value), so restricting
//@ reason: throw to a single bare operand was an arbitrary gap, not a safety rule.
//@ run: bad path: x.yaml really
//
// Found while building yaml-cling's LoadFile (`throw "bad file: " + path;`) --
// see notes/GAPS.md in yaml-cling for the motivating real-world call site.

int main() {
    string path = "x.yaml";
    string suffix = "really";
    try {
        throw "bad path: " + path + " " + suffix;
    } catch (string e) {
        std::cout << e << std::endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
